Epididymitis
Young Adult & Adolecence
Gradual onset unilateral scrotal pain, Localized epididymal tenderness, Dysuria/urethral discharge, Increased epididymal vascularity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-016
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of unilateral scrotal pain and swelling accompanied by dysuria and systemic malaise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a rapidly worsening unilateral scrotal swelling, severe localized pain, and dysuria. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to exclude an acute surgical emergency, specifically testicular torsion, and to initiate immediate targeted antimicrobial therapy for suspected epididymitis to prevent abscess formation and potential ischemic necrosis prior to the availability of the patient's regular general practitioner.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My right testicle has been aching for two days, but tonight it swelled up like a balloon and it burns like fire when I try to pee."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 48-hour history of gradual onset unilateral posterior scrotal pain and swelling. Initially manifesting as a dull ache, the pain has progressively intensified over the past 12 hours. The patient reports concomitant dysuria and increased urinary frequency. Within the last 6 hours, the patient noted a mucopurulent urethral discharge and the onset of a low-grade fever accompanied by systemic malaise. The scrotal skin overlying the affected area has become distinctly erythematous and warm to the touch. The pain is exacerbated by ambulation and somewhat relieved by lying supine.
Functional Status: Ambulating with a wide-based gait to minimize scrotal friction; severe discomfort limiting normal daily activities.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient lives alone; high anxiety regarding potential loss of the testicle and future fertility implications.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/82 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient appears flushed, mildly diaphoretic, and visibly uncomfortable, frequently adjusting his position while seated.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary examination (chaperoned) reveals marked right-sided scrotal erythema and oedema. Palpation demonstrates a gradual onset unilateral scrotal pain that is exquisitely localized to the posterior epididymis. The testicle itself is normally oriented. There is a positive Prehn's sign, characterized by pain relief upon manual elevation of the affected hemiscrotum. An intact ipsilateral cremasteric reflex is elicited. A small reactive hydrocele is present on the affected side. Milking the urethra yields a scant purulent discharge, consistent with the dysuria/urethral discharge presentation. Clinical presentation strongly warrants a scrotal Doppler ultrasound to confirm the expected increased epididymal vascularity and rule out torsion.
Environmental Observations: Patient has rapid access to a bathroom; living environment is safe and hygienic.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient took Paracetamol 1000mg 3 hours ago with minimal analgesic effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Unilateral Epididymitis (Suspected STI etiology) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Testicular Torsion - Ruled out/unlikely due to the gradual rather than hyperacute onset of pain, the presence of a positive cremasteric reflex, and pain relief upon scrotal elevation (Prehn's sign).

Orchitis (Mumps) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain being strictly localized to the epididymis rather than the testicular body, and the absence of parotid gland swelling or recent viral prodrome.

Incarcerated Inguinal Hernia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of a palpable mass extending into the inguinal canal and normal, active bowel sounds with no signs of intestinal obstruction.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits localized epididymal tenderness, urethral discharge, and a positive Prehn's sign, establishing a clinical diagnosis of acute epididymitis. The progressive nature of the severe scrotal pain and swelling necessitated an urgent after-hours home visit to definitively differentiate this condition from an acute testicular torsion, which would require immediate surgical exploration within a 6-hour window to save the testicle. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of misdiagnosing ischemia or allowing the epididymal infection to progress to abscess formation. Immediate clinical evaluation safely excluded torsion and allowed for the prompt initiation of empiric antibiotic therapy and pain management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ceftriaxone 500mg IM stat and prescribed Doxycycline 100mg PO BID for 14 days (empiric coverage for Chlamydia and Gonorrhoea). Ordered an urgent first-catch urine for NAAT and a formal scrotal ultrasound to be completed the following morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically reduce the intense scrotal swelling and pain, wear supportive, tight-fitting athletic underwear constantly, directly utilizing mechanical elevation to decrease vascular engorgement and relieve tension on the spermatic cord. To ensure complete eradication of the infection, set a daily phone alarm to take the Doxycycline with a full glass of water, explicitly linking the routine to rapid pain resolution and preventing long-term fertility issues.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 48 hours to assess clinical response, review ultrasound and NAAT results, and conduct necessary contact tracing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the scrotal pain suddenly becomes agonizing and unbearable, if the testicle appears to physically ride higher in the scrotum, or if a high fever accompanied by rigors develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinically presumed pending formal ultrasound to definitively exclude torsion and urine tests to identify the specific bacteria.
Clinical Course: The acute pain and swelling will begin to subside within 48 to 72 hours of antibiotic initiation, but mild aching may persist for several weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if your pain suddenly spikes to an unbearable level, if your testicle suddenly changes position and sits higher up, or if you develop uncontrollable shivering and a high fever.

